These three polynomials are pairwise Seidel-compatible but we find that there is only one possible solution to \eqref{eq:sumofsubpoly_dim17Compat}: $(91/2,1,5/2)$, which is a contradiction.
On the other hand, only four out of the 53 polynomials are Seidel-compatible with $\mathfrak{f}_4(x)$:
\begin{align*}
    \mathfrak{f}_1(x) &= (x+5)^{31} (x-9)^{11} (x-11)^2 (x^4-34x^3+404x^2-1966x+3291), \\
    \mathfrak{f}_4(x) &= (x+5)^{31} (x-9)^{11} (x-11)^2 (x^4-34x^3+404x^2-1966x+3339), \\
    \mathfrak{f}_{24}(x) &= (x+5)^{31} (x-9)^{11} (x-11)^2 (x^4-34x^3+404x^2-1942x+3075), \\
